def GlobalDocumentation {
  code Intro =[{..
  -------------------------------------------------------------------
  NOTE: This file is automatically generated by running llvm-tblgen
  -gen-kitsune-loop-attr-docs. Do not edit this file by hand!!
  -------------------------------------------------------------------

=============================
LLVM Loop Attribute Reference
=============================
.. contents::
   :local:

.. |br| raw:: html

  <br/>

These are Kitsune-specific "attributes" that may be attached to LLVM loops.
They are actually added to LLVM's loop metadata. Each attribute appears in the
metadata as a pair consisting of a string that identifies the attribute and a
value for the attribute.

Kitsune's loop attributes must be one of the following kinds.

* **Flag Attributes**: These are attributes that do not take a value. Only
  their presence or absence is significant. Despite this, if any are attached to
  a loop, they will always have the value ``i32 1`` in the loop metadata.

* **Valued Attributes**: These are attributes that take a value. Currently,
  only certain types of values are supported. These, together with the
  corresponding type that will appear in the LLVM-IR loop metadata are
  summarized in the table below.

  =============  ============
  Source type    LLVM-IR type
  =============  ============
  ``int32_t``    ``i32``
  ``int64_t``    ``i64``
  ``StringRef``  ``[n x i8]``
  =============  ============

* **Enum Attributes**: These are a special case of the valued attributes
  where the value type is an **enum** defined in Kitsune's source. The
  type of the value in LLVM-IR metadata will always be ``i32``.

In the example below, the loop metadata is contained in metadata node ``!0``.
Three attributes have been attached to the loop,
:ref:`tapir.loop.lowering.enabled <loop-attr-lowering-enabled>`,
:ref:`tapir.loop.perfect.depth <loop-attr-perfect-depth>`, and
:ref:`tapir.loop.target <loop-attr-target>`.

.. code :: kitll

  define void @f(i64 %n) {
  entry:
    %syncreg = tail call token @llvm.syncregion.start()
    %cmp.not = icmp eq i64 %n, 0
    br i1 %cmp.not, label %for.i.sync, label %for.i.header

  for.i.header:
    %i = phi i64 [ 0, %entry ], [ %inc.i, %for.i.latch ]
    detach within %syncreg, label %for.i.body, label %for.i.latch

  for.i.body:
    reattach within %syncreg, label %for.i.latch

  for.i.latch:
    %inc.i = add i64 %i, 1
    %exitcond.i.not = icmp eq i64 %inc.i, %n
    br i1 %exitcond.i.not, label %for.i.sync, label %for.i.header, !llvm.loop !0

  for.i.sync:
    sync within %syncreg, label %for.i.exit

  for.i.exit:
    ret void
  }

  !0 = distinct !{!0, !1, !2, !3}
  !1 = !{!"tapir.loop.lowering.enabled", i32 1}
  !2 = !{!"tapir.loop.perfect.depth", i32 3}
  !3 = !{!"tapir.loop.target", i32 1024}

Here, ``tapir.loop.perfect.depth`` is a valued attribute and therefore, the
acutal value of the attribute is 3. However ``tapir.loop.target`` is an
enum attribute. In this case, the value is of type ``TTID``, and the integer
value of 1024 corresponds to ``TTID::Pthreads``.

Every attribute has a corresponding entry in the ``LoopAttrKind`` enum. The
names of these enums are provided in the documentation for each attribute.

}];
}

include "kitsune/Core/LoopAttrs.td"
